    <span class="section-label">Return on investment</span>
    <h1>What a website should <em>earn for your business</em></h1>
    <p>Sooner or later, every owner asks the same question: <em>if I spend $2,100 a year on the site, what do I get back?</em> The answer depends on the math, and this page lays it out: four things a well-built site improves, a worked example for a typical local business, and two ways to check it against your own business.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="text-center" style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto">
      <span class="section-label">The framing</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">A website is an asset that grows over time</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="roi-intro" style="max-width:72ch;margin:1.75rem auto 0">
      <p>Owners usually think about a website in one of two ways. Some see an expense, like office rent, and buy the cheapest one. Others see a growth engine that prints money, which treats it like a slot machine. Neither matches how a small-business website behaves.</p>
      <p>A service-business website gains value slowly when it's built and looked after well, and loses value slowly when it isn't. The return isn't dramatic in any one month. It's the difference between a phone that rings four times a week and one that rings eight times, added up over years. Measured month to month, it seems flat. Measured year to year, you'll see the curve.</p>
      <p>The rest of this page puts numbers on that idea.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--mist">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="text-center" style="max-width:760px;margin:0 auto">
      <span class="section-label">The four levers</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">Four things a well-built site does better</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle" style="margin-inline:auto">You can measure each of these. Each one also works on its own, so you can fix one without the others, but fixing all four is what adds up over a year.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="roi-levers">
      <li class="roi-lever">
        <span class="roi-lever-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
        <h3>Speed keeps visitors who would otherwise leave</h3>
        <p>Google's own research has said the same thing for most of a decade: roughly half of mobile visitors leave a page that takes longer than three seconds to load, and more leave with each extra second. A site that scores 55 on Google's speed test isn't losing customers because it looks bad. It's losing them because they never see it. They tap the back button before the first screen finishes loading.</p>
        <p class="roi-lever-foot">In rough numbers, moving from a speed score of 55 to 95 or higher typically wins back 25&ndash;40% of the mobile visitors who would otherwise have left. For a business with 1,000 monthly visitors from search, 70% of them on phones, that's 175&ndash;280 more visitors a month who see the site.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="roi-lever">
        <span class="roi-lever-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        <h3>Local search decides whether customers find you on Google</h3>
        <p>For a service business, ranking on Google mostly means appearing in Google's map results (the &ldquo;local pack&rdquo;), the panel of three businesses with a map at the top of local searches. Being in it brings several times more calls than being just below it. The placement comes from things you can measure: matching business details across the web, labels on your site that tell Google what you do, a complete Google Business Profile, a steady flow of new reviews, and thorough service-area pages. Each one is a checklist item.</p>
        <p class="roi-lever-foot">With local search groundwork in place from launch, a site typically appears in the local pack within 60&ndash;90 days. Without it, a site can stay invisible for years. That's the difference between a website that pays for itself and a brochure no one finds.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="roi-lever">
        <span class="roi-lever-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
        <h3>A clear path to contact decides whether visitors call</h3>
        <p>Every page on a service-business site should answer one question: <em>how do I reach this business right now?</em> That means your phone number on the first screen of every page, a working form that asks the right questions, tap-to-call and tap-to-text on phones, and a thank-you page that explains what happens next. Together, they turn a visitor into a lead.</p>
        <p class="roi-lever-foot">When that path is clear, a typical service-business site turns 2&ndash;4% of visitors from search into a call or a form. When it isn't, the same site converts a fraction of that, because the people who came couldn't tell what to do.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="roi-lever">
        <span class="roi-lever-num" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
        <h3>Steady upkeep keeps the other three from slipping</h3>
        <p>Every site ages, and neglected ones age badly. A plugin-based site with no upkeep collects plugin conflicts, security holes, and broken pages within 12&ndash;18 months. A site on a do-it-yourself builder won't break the same way, but it won't get faster or rank better either, while competitors who keep up their sites pass yours. The cost of neglect is invisible in any one month, which is why it gets ignored and why most small-business sites slowly lose ground.</p>
        <p class="roi-lever-foot">A maintained site keeps its speed score, its place in the local pack, its working forms, and a steady flow of fresh content. That doesn't take daily attention. It takes monthly attention from someone who knows what to look for.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section roi-example">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:980px">
    <span class="section-label">The worked example</span>
    <h2 class="section-title" style="max-width:30ch">The worked example: a typical local service business</h2>

    <p style="max-width:72ch">These figures are a worked example for a typical local business, and every business is different. They show the <em>shape</em> of the math rather than a dollar amount for your situation, so plug in your own numbers as you read.</p>

    <h3>Starting assumptions</h3>
    <ul class="roi-stats">
      <li class="roi-stat"><span class="roi-stat-num">1,000</span><span class="roi-stat-label">Monthly visitors from search</span></li>
      <li class="roi-stat"><span class="roi-stat-num">70%</span><span class="roi-stat-label">Share on phones</span></li>
      <li class="roi-stat"><span class="roi-stat-num">$400</span><span class="roi-stat-label">Average job value</span></li>
      <li class="roi-stat"><span class="roi-stat-num">1.2%</span><span class="roi-stat-label">Visitors who get in touch today</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="max-width:72ch;color:var(--text-muted);font-size:0.92rem">The example is a residential service business (a plumber, electrician, HVAC company, roofer, landscaper, or inspector) in a metro of 100,000&ndash;500,000 people. Its current site is a do-it-yourself template that scores 55 on Google's speed test, with no labels for search engines and a Google Business Profile that's set up but rarely updated.</p>

    <h3>Before and after, 12 months apart</h3>
    <p style="max-width:72ch">Same traffic sources, same metro, same job value. Different site.</p>

    <div class="roi-ba">
      <article class="roi-ba-card roi-ba--before">
        <span class="roi-ba-eyebrow">Current site &middot; today</span>
        <h4>What the current site loses today</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Speed score <strong>55</strong>, with <strong>35&ndash;45%</strong> of phone visitors leaving before the page loads.</li>
          <li>245&ndash;315 phone visitors a month never see the site.</li>
          <li>About <strong>$1,200&ndash;$1,500 a month lost to slow loading alone</strong>.</li>
          <li>Missing from the local pack, which typically means <strong>30&ndash;50% fewer calls</strong> than a business that appears in it.</li>
          <li>Only <strong>1.2%</strong> of visitors get in touch, because the path from visitor to phone call is unclear.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="roi-ba-card roi-ba--after">
        <span class="roi-ba-eyebrow">Built well &middot; month 12</span>
        <h4>What changes on the same traffic by month 12</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Speed score <strong>95+</strong>, with under <strong>10%</strong> of phone visitors leaving before the page loads.</li>
          <li>A place in the local pack for your main service-and-city searches within <strong>60&ndash;90 days</strong>.</li>
          <li>Visits from search up <strong>40&ndash;80%</strong> as rankings for more specific searches build up.</li>
          <li><strong>2.5&ndash;3.5%</strong> of visitors get in touch as the path to contact gets clearer.</li>
          <li>Combined effect: <strong>2&ndash;4&times; the monthly calls</strong> by month 12.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="roi-bottomline">
      <span class="roi-bottomline-eyebrow">The bottom line</span>
      <p>At a $400 average job and even a modest close rate, the site's yearly cost typically comes to <strong>3&ndash;8% of the extra revenue the site brings in</strong>. In this worked example, that's a return of 12 to 30 times the cost, sustained year after year rather than in a single good quarter.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

