<section class="section section--mist">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:780px">
    <span class="section-label">Where the math has limits</span>
    <h2 class="section-title" style="max-width:30ch">The limits of a good website</h2>

    <p style="max-width:72ch">The math above assumes a few things that aren't always true. Here's each one.</p>

    <ul class="roi-caveats">
      <li><strong>A good website can't fix a business customers don't want to hire.</strong>If the service is overpriced, slow to respond, or has a poor local reputation, the site will pass that on to everyone who finds it. The site amplifies the business; it doesn't replace it.</li>
      <li><strong>A good website can't outrank a market leader with a 15-year head start.</strong>It can make you competitive where the field is thin or where the leaders have neglected their own sites. Against strong, well-kept competitors, the site narrows the gap, but it won't close it alone.</li>
      <li><strong>A good website can't replace the offline marketing you already have.</strong>Vehicle wraps, yard signs, referrals, and community involvement all still matter. The site makes them stronger by being the place every offline contact eventually points to.</li>
      <li><strong>A good website can't produce a return in month one.</strong>The numbers above describe 12 months of growth. Most of the curve comes after month three. The first 60 days are mostly Google finding and indexing the site, which is slow to watch but does the work.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="max-width:72ch;margin-top:1.75rem">These limits aren't reasons to skip a good site. They're reasons to expect the right things from it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:880px">
    <span class="section-label">Check it yourself</span>
    <h2 class="section-title" style="max-width:30ch">Two ways to check the math against your business</h2>

    <p style="max-width:72ch">This page describes a typical business. Yours may be better or worse, and the only way to know is to measure. You can take both steps yourself, without hiring anyone:</p>

    <ol class="roi-verify">
      <li>
        <div>
          <strong>Run your site through Google's speed test.</strong>
          <p>Go to <a href="https://pagespeed.web.dev" rel="noopener" class="inline-link">pagespeed.web.dev</a>, paste your site's address, and click Analyze. The phone score is the one that matters; the computer score is usually higher and tells you less. If your phone score is below 80, speed is holding your site back, and the math above roughly applies to you.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <strong>Search for your main service and city on a phone, away from your business address.</strong>
          <p>If your business shows up in the local pack (the block of three listings with the map), local search is mostly working for you. If it doesn't show, or shows only after scrolling, local search is almost certainly your biggest opportunity.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

